<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Entitlements for the containing app.

  Authority: ADR-0016 §11.2's hardening table's iOS/iPadOS row; ADR-0016 §11.4
  (the declared residual); ADR-0020 §11.3's iOS row and ST-30.

  The app needs `vpn.api` to present the consent flow (ADR-0016 §11.2's iOS
  table: "`NETunnelProviderManager` configuration and VPN-profile installation |
  App | Only the app can present the consent flow"), and it shares the keychain
  access group and the App Group with the extension because the two are one app
  identity.

  ===========================================================================
  WHAT THE SHARING COSTS, DECLARED RATHER THAN GLOSSED
  ===========================================================================

  ADR-0016 §11.4's compromised-process table, iOS/iPadOS column: an attacker in
  the APP process can "use DeviceKey as signing oracle = **Yes — declared
  residual**", because `kSecAttrAccessGroup` cannot be scoped below app identity.
  It cannot: obtain the tun descriptor, write the firewall, change routes or the
  resolver, disarm enforcement, inject code into the provider, or export the
  DeviceKey's private material — those are all OS-enforced.

  PS-24 condition 4 states the consequence in terms: PS-1's "sole holder of the
  key handle" clause is, "on HC-2 only, a declared residual rather than an
  enforceable property, tested as a declaration in P16."

  ADR-0020 ST-30's single-opener rule is likewise a DISCIPLINE here and not a
  sandbox: the app holds the App Group entitlement and MUST NOT open the vault.
  PS-24 condition 2's per-fact writer table is what makes that checkable.

  ===========================================================================
  THE SHARED KEYCHAIN GROUP IS THE APP GROUP, AND IS NOT LISTED SEPARATELY
  ===========================================================================

  This file used to carry

      <key>keychain-access-groups</key>
      <array><string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)group.net.twinvpn.client</string></array>

  and it was wrong twice over. Apple, "Sharing access to keychain items among a
  collection of apps": the system ALREADY treats every app-group identifier as a
  keychain access group, so listing one adds nothing; and app-group identifiers
  are NOT team-prefixed the way keychain groups are, so the
  `$(AppIdentifierPrefix)` was a prefix on a name that must not carry one.

  The consequence was invisible to every check this repository runs. A simulator
  build ignores entitlements, `swiftc -parse` never opens this file, and nothing
  on a Linux host can sign anything — so it would have failed for the first time
  on a signed device, as `errSecMissingEntitlement` from the first keychain call.

  What the two processes share is therefore `com.apple.security.application-
  groups` below, and that one entitlement carries both the container and the
  keychain group. ADR-0016 §11.2's "shared keychain-access-groups and App Group
  for app+extension" is satisfied by it, not weakened: the sharing is unchanged
  and only its spelling is.

  STATUS: written, not compiled and not signed.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.developer.networking.vpn.api</key>
    <array>
        <string>allow-vpn</string>
    </array>

    <key>com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension</key>
    <array>
        <string>packet-tunnel-provider</string>
    </array>

    <!-- The App Group. It is ALSO the shared keychain access group — see the
         header: the system adds every app-group identifier to the keychain
         access group list, un-prefixed, without it being declared. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
    <array>
        <string>group.net.twinvpn.client</string>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
